    <div class="entry-content" set:html={`<p data-start="345" data-end="708">Ancient grains have become a staple for health-conscious consumers. You might have come across questions like, “is quinoa and korralu same?” In this post, we break down both grains in detail—from their origins and varieties to nutritional profiles, cooking methods, and health benefits. Let’s explore what sets these two apart and how each can fit into your diet.</p><hr data-start="710" data-end="713" /><h2 data-start="715" data-end="736"><img src="/images/Is-Quinoa-and-Korralu-Same-21-300x210.jpg" decoding="async" class="wp-image-953 aligncenter" alt="Is Quinoa and Korralu Same " width="549" height="384" title="Is Quinoa and Korralu Same? A Deep Comparison of Two Ancient Grains 3" /></h2><div id="ez-toc-container" class="ez-toc-v2_0_85 counter-hierarchy ez-toc-counter ez-toc-grey ez-toc-container-direction"><div class="ez-toc-title-container"><p class="ez-toc-title" style="cursor:inherit">Table of Contents</p>
<span class="ez-toc-title-toggle"><a href="#" class="ez-toc-pull-right ez-toc-btn ez-toc-btn-xs ez-toc-btn-default ez-toc-toggle" aria-label="Toggle Table of Content"><span class="ez-toc-js-icon-con"><span class=""><span class="eztoc-hide" style="display:none;">Toggle</span><span class="ez-toc-icon-toggle-span"><svg style="fill: #999;color:#999" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="list-377408" width="20px" height="20px" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none"><path d="M6 6H4v2h2V6zm14 0H8v2h12V6zM4 11h2v2H4v-2zm16 0H8v2h12v-2zM4 16h2v2H4v-2zm16 0H8v2h12v-2z" fill="currentColor"></path></svg><svg style="fill: #999;color:#999" class="arrow-unsorted-368013" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="10px" height="10px" viewBox="0 0 24 24" version="1.2" baseProfile="tiny"><path d="M18.2 9.3l-6.2-6.3-6.2 6.3c-.2.2-.3.4-.3.7s.1.5.3.7c.2.2.4.3.7.3h11c.3 0 .5-.1.7-.3.2-.2.3-.5.3-.7s-.1-.5-.3-.7zM5.8 14.7l6.2 6.3 6.2-6.3c.2-.2.3-.5.3-.7s-.1-.5-.3-.7c-.2-.2-.4-.3-.7-.3h-11c-.3 0-.5.1-.7.3-.2.2-.3.5-.3.7s.1.5.3.7z"/></svg></span></span></span></a></span></div><nav><ul class='ez-toc-list ez-toc-list-level-1 ' ><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-1" href="#1_What_Is_Quinoa" >1. What Is Quinoa?</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-2" href="#2_What_Is_Korralu" >2. What Is Korralu?</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-3" href="#3_Why_Compare_Quinoa_and_Korralu" >3. Why Compare Quinoa and Korralu?</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-4" href="#4_Nutritional_Comparison_Quinoa_vs_Korralu" >4. Nutritional Comparison: Quinoa vs. Korralu</a><ul class='ez-toc-list-level-3' ><li class='ez-toc-heading-level-3'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-5" href="#Nutritional_Analysis" >Nutritional Analysis</a></li></ul></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-6" href="#5_Cooking_and_Usage_Scenarios" >5. Cooking and Usage Scenarios</a><ul class='ez-toc-list-level-3' ><li class='ez-toc-heading-level-3'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-7" href="#Quinoa_When_and_How_to_Use_It" >Quinoa: When and How to Use It</a></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-3'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-8" href="#Korralu_When_and_How_to_Use_It" >Korralu: When and How to Use It</a></li></ul></li><li class='ez-toc-page-1 ez-toc-heading-level-2'><a class="ez-toc-link ez-toc-heading-9" href="#6_Health_Recommendations_Scientific_Evidence" >6. Health Recommendations &amp; Scientific Evidence</a></li></ul></nav></div><h2 data-start="715" data-end="736"><span class="ez-toc-section" id="1_What_Is_Quinoa"></span>1. What Is Quinoa?<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2><p data-start="738" data-end="887">Quinoa (<em data-start="746" data-end="766">Chenopodium quinoa</em>) is often called a “pseudo-cereal” because it’s not a true grain but is used similarly. Here are the key specifications:</p><ul data-start="889" data-end="2036"><li data-start="889" data-end="1095"><p data-start="891" data-end="912"><strong data-start="891" data-end="912">Origin &amp; History:</strong></p><ul data-start="915" data-end="1095"><li data-start="915" data-end="988"><strong data-start="917" data-end="935">Native Region:</strong> South America (Andes region: Peru, Bolivia, Ecuador)</li><li data-start="991" data-end="1095"><strong data-start="993" data-end="1021">Historical Significance:</strong> Revered by the Incas as the “mother grain” and used in ceremonial dishes.</li></ul></li><li data-start="1097" data-end="1353"><p data-start="1099" data-end="1113"><strong data-start="1099" data-end="1113">Varieties:</strong></p><ul data-start="1116" data-end="1353"><li data-start="1116" data-end="1174"><strong data-start="1118" data-end="1135">White Quinoa:</strong> Most common; tender and mild-flavored.</li><li data-start="1177" data-end="1260"><strong data-start="1179" data-end="1194">Red Quinoa:</strong> Has a slightly chewier texture; ideal for salads and cold dishes.</li><li data-start="1263" data-end="1353"><strong data-start="1265" data-end="1282">Black Quinoa:</strong> Darker color with a stronger, earthier taste; requires longer cooking.</li></ul></li><li data-start="1355" data-end="1618"><p data-start="1357" data-end="1388"><strong data-start="1357" data-end="1388">Growing Conditions &amp; Yield:</strong></p><ul data-start="1391" data-end="1618"><li data-start="1391" data-end="1457"><strong data-start="1393" data-end="1405">Climate:</strong> Thrives in high-altitude, cool, and dry conditions.</li><li data-start="1460" data-end="1518"><strong data-start="1462" data-end="1471">Soil:</strong> Adapts well to poor soils with minimal inputs.</li><li data-start="1521" data-end="1618"><strong data-start="1523" data-end="1533">Yield:</strong> Approximately 1–2 tons per hectare, although yields vary with cultivation practices.</li></ul></li><li data-start="1620" data-end="1912"><p data-start="1622" data-end="1649"><strong data-start="1622" data-end="1649">Uses &amp; Cooking Methods:</strong></p><ul data-start="1652" data-end="1912"><li data-start="1652" data-end="1733"><strong data-start="1654" data-end="1666">Rinsing:</strong> Must be rinsed thoroughly to remove natural saponins (bitterness).</li><li data-start="1736" data-end="1810"><strong data-start="1738" data-end="1750">Cooking:</strong> Use a water-to-quinoa ratio of 2:1; boil for 15–20 minutes.</li><li data-start="1813" data-end="1912"><strong data-start="1815" data-end="1832">Applications:</strong> Ideal in salads, as a rice substitute, in porridges, or even baked into snacks.</li></ul></li><li data-start="1914" data-end="2036"><p data-start="1916" data-end="1936"><strong data-start="1916" data-end="1936">Popular Regions:</strong></p><ul data-start="1939" data-end="2036"><li data-start="1939" data-end="2036">Gaining popularity in North America, Europe, and parts of Asia due to its nutritional benefits.</li></ul></li></ul><hr data-start="2038" data-end="2041" /><h2 data-start="2043" data-end="2065"><span class="ez-toc-section" id="2_What_Is_Korralu"></span>2. What Is Korralu?<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2><p data-start="2067" data-end="2236">Korralu is a term used in some regions to describe a type of millet or small grain. Although less globally recognized than quinoa, it has its own set of unique features:</p><ul data-start="2238" data-end="3386"><li data-start="2238" data-end="2489"><p data-start="2240" data-end="2261"><strong data-start="2240" data-end="2261">Origin &amp; History:</strong></p><ul data-start="2264" data-end="2489"><li data-start="2264" data-end="2381"><strong data-start="2266" data-end="2284">Native Region:</strong> Primarily grown in parts of Asia (notably India and Southeast Asia) and in some African regions.</li><li data-start="2384" data-end="2489"><strong data-start="2386" data-end="2405">Historical Use:</strong> Traditional staple in rural diets, used for both daily meals and festive occasions.</li></ul></li><li data-start="2491" data-end="2737"><p data-start="2493" data-end="2507"><strong data-start="2493" data-end="2507">Varieties:</strong></p><ul data-start="2510" data-end="2737"><li data-start="2510" data-end="2600"><strong data-start="2512" data-end="2533">Standard Korralu:</strong> Often characterized by small, golden grains used as a main cereal.</li><li data-start="2603" data-end="2737"><strong data-start="2605" data-end="2646">Improved/Drought-Resistant Varieties:</strong> Developed through local breeding programs to enhance yield in arid and semi-arid climates.</li></ul></li><li data-start="2739" data-end="2963"><p data-start="2741" data-end="2772"><strong data-start="2741" data-end="2772">Growing Conditions &amp; Yield:</strong></p><ul data-start="2775" data-end="2963"><li data-start="2775" data-end="2852"><strong data-start="2777" data-end="2789">Climate:</strong> Prefers warm, dry environments and is highly drought-tolerant.</li><li data-start="2855" data-end="2963"><strong data-start="2857" data-end="2867">Yield:</strong> Typically around 0.8–1.5 tons per hectare, depending on local conditions and farming practices.</li></ul></li><li data-start="2965" data-end="3276"><p data-start="2967" data-end="2994"><strong data-start="2967" data-end="2994">Uses &amp; Cooking Methods:</strong></p><ul data-start="2997" data-end="3276"><li data-start="2997" data-end="3061"><strong data-start="2999" data-end="3018">Direct Cooking:</strong> Can be cooked similarly to rice or millet.</li><li data-start="3064" data-end="3152"><strong data-start="3066" data-end="3078">Milling:</strong> Often ground into flour for making flatbreads, porridges, or baked goods.</li><li data-start="3155" data-end="3276"><strong data-start="3157" data-end="3174">Applications:</strong> Commonly used in traditional dishes, breakfast porridges, and even as a base for fermented beverages.</li></ul></li><li data-start="3278" data-end="3386"><p data-start="3280" data-end="3300"><strong data-start="3280" data-end="3300">Popular Regions:</strong></p><ul data-start="3303" data-end="3386"><li data-start="3303" data-end="3386">Widely consumed in India, parts of Southeast Asia, and rural African communities.</li></ul></li></ul><hr data-start="3388" data-end="3391" /><h2 data-start="3393" data-end="3430"><span class="ez-toc-section" id="3_Why_Compare_Quinoa_and_Korralu"></span>3. Why Compare Quinoa and Korralu?<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2><p data-start="3432" data-end="3501">There are several reasons you might compare these two ancient grains:</p><ul data-start="3503" data-end="4265"><li data-start="3503" data-end="3672"><p data-start="3505" data-end="3534"><strong data-start="3505" data-end="3534">Gluten-Free Alternatives:</strong></p><ul data-start="3537" data-end="3672"><li data-start="3537" data-end="3672">Both quinoa and korralu are naturally gluten-free, making them excellent choices for those with celiac disease or gluten sensitivity.</li></ul></li><li data-start="3674" data-end="4020"><p data-start="3676" data-end="3717"><strong data-start="3676" data-end="3717">Nutritional and Functional Diversity:</strong></p><ul data-start="3720" data-end="4020"><li data-start="3720" data-end="3809"><strong data-start="3722" data-end="3732">Quinoa</strong> is renowned for its complete protein content and a robust array of minerals.</li><li data-start="3812" data-end="3919"><strong data-start="3814" data-end="3825">Korralu</strong> offers high fiber and low fat, which can be beneficial for digestion and blood sugar control.</li><li data-start="3922" data-end="4020">Comparing them helps you tailor your diet based on your nutritional needs and taste preferences.</li></ul></li><li data-start="4022" data-end="4265"><p data-start="4024" data-end="4056"><strong data-start="4024" data-end="4056">Cultural &amp; Culinary Variety:</strong></p><ul data-start="4059" data-end="4265"><li data-start="4059" data-end="4128">They represent different culinary traditions and farming practices.</li><li data-start="4131" data-end="4265">For those exploring ancient grains, understanding the nuances can help in incorporating a diverse range of nutrients into your diet.</li></ul></li></ul><hr data-start="4267" data-end="4270" /><h2 data-start="4272" data-end="4320"><span class="ez-toc-section" id="4_Nutritional_Comparison_Quinoa_vs_Korralu"></span>4. Nutritional Comparison: Quinoa vs. Korralu<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2><p data-start="4322" data-end="4462">Below is a table comparing approximate nutritional values per 100 grams of raw grain. (Data source: USDA and various food science journals.)</p><div class="overflow-x-auto contain-inline-size"><table border="1" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="2" data-start="4464" data-end="5102"><thead data-start="4464" data-end="4534"><tr data-start="4464" data-end="4534"><th data-start="4464" data-end="4485"><strong data-start="4466" data-end="4478">Nutrient</strong></th><th data-start="4485" data-end="4508"><strong data-start="4487" data-end="4497">Quinoa</strong></th><th data-start="4508" data-end="4534"><strong data-start="4510" data-end="4521">Korralu</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody data-start="4606" data-end="5102"><tr data-start="4606" data-end="4676"><td><strong data-start="4608" data-end="4620">Calories</strong></td><td>368 kcal</td><td>351 kcal</td></tr><tr data-start="4677" data-end="4747"><td><strong data-start="4679" data-end="4690">Protein</strong></td><td>14 g</td><td>11 g</td></tr><tr data-start="4748" data-end="4818"><td><strong data-start="4750" data-end="4757">Fat</strong></td><td>6 g</td><td>3.5 g</td></tr><tr data-start="4819" data-end="4889"><td><strong data-start="4821" data-end="4838">Carbohydrates</strong></td><td>64 g</td><td>67 g</td></tr><tr data-start="4890" data-end="4960"><td><strong data-start="4892" data-end="4909">Dietary Fiber</strong></td><td>7 g</td><td>8 g</td></tr><tr data-start="4961" data-end="5031"><td><strong data-start="4963" data-end="4971">Iron</strong></td><td>2.8 mg</td><td>2.2 mg</td></tr><tr data-start="5032" data-end="5102"><td><strong data-start="5034" data-end="5047">Magnesium</strong></td><td>197 mg</td><td>~90 mg</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 data-start="5104" data-end="5128"><span class="ez-toc-section" id="Nutritional_Analysis"></span>Nutritional Analysis<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h3><ul data-start="5130" data-end="5809"><li data-start="5130" data-end="5412"><p data-start="5132" data-end="5143"><strong data-start="5132" data-end="5143">Quinoa:</strong></p><ul data-start="5146" data-end="5412"><li data-start="5146" data-end="5270"><strong data-start="5148" data-end="5163">Advantages:</strong><ul data-start="5168" data-end="5270"><li data-start="5168" data-end="5221">High in protein with a complete amino acid profile.</li><li data-start="5226" data-end="5270">Rich in magnesium, iron, and antioxidants.</li></ul></li><li data-start="5273" data-end="5412"><strong data-start="5275" data-end="5293">Disadvantages:</strong><ul data-start="5298" data-end="5412"><li data-start="5298" data-end="5412">Slightly higher in calories and fat, which might require portion control if you’re watching your caloric intake.</li></ul></li></ul></li><li data-start="5414" data-end="5809"><p data-start="5416" data-end="5428"><strong data-start="5416" data-end="5428">Korralu:</strong></p><ul data-start="5431" data-end="5809"><li data-start="5431" data-end="5599"><strong data-start="5433" data-end="5448">Advantages:</strong><ul data-start="5453" data-end="5599"><li data-start="5453" data-end="5502">Lower in fat and a bit higher in dietary fiber.</li><li data-start="5507" data-end="5599">Often associated with a lower glycemic index, making it favorable for blood sugar control.</li></ul></li><li data-start="5602" data-end="5809"><strong data-start="5604" data-end="5622">Disadvantages:</strong><ul data-start="5627" data-end="5809"><li data-start="5627" data-end="5707">Lower protein content and lacks some essential amino acids compared to quinoa.</li><li data-start="5712" data-end="5809">Contains fewer minerals like magnesium, necessitating a balanced diet with complementary foods.</li></ul></li></ul></li></ul><hr data-start="5811" data-end="5814" /><h2 data-start="5816" data-end="5849"><span class="ez-toc-section" id="5_Cooking_and_Usage_Scenarios"></span>5. Cooking and Usage Scenarios<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2><p data-start="5851" data-end="5967">Below are various hypothetical scenarios to help you decide which grain might suit your lifestyle and dietary needs.</p><h3 data-start="5969" data-end="6003"><span class="ez-toc-section" id="Quinoa_When_and_How_to_Use_It"></span>Quinoa: When and How to Use It<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h3><ul data-start="6005" data-end="6664"><li data-start="6005" data-end="6228"><p data-start="6007" data-end="6027"><strong data-start="6007" data-end="6027">Breakfast Boost:</strong></p><ul data-start="6030" data-end="6228"><li data-start="6030" data-end="6142"><strong data-start="6032" data-end="6045">Scenario:</strong> If you’re rushing in the morning, prepare a batch of quinoa porridge mixed with fruits and nuts.</li><li data-start="6145" data-end="6228"><strong data-start="6147" data-end="6155">Tip:</strong> Rinse quinoa well, boil for 15 minutes, and top with a drizzle of honey.</li></ul></li><li data-start="6230" data-end="6449"><p data-start="6232" data-end="6250"><strong data-start="6232" data-end="6250">Salad Upgrade:</strong></p><ul data-start="6253" data-end="6449"><li data-start="6253" data-end="6371"><strong data-start="6255" data-end="6268">Scenario:</strong> For a refreshing lunch, toss cooked quinoa with chopped vegetables, olive oil, lemon juice, and herbs.</li><li data-start="6374" data-end="6449"><strong data-start="6376" data-end="6384">Tip:</strong> Its nutty flavor enhances the taste of fresh greens and avocado.</li></ul></li><li data-start="6451" data-end="6664"><p data-start="6453" data-end="6482"><strong data-start="6453" data-end="6482">Side Dish or Main Course:</strong></p><ul data-start="6485" data-end="6664"><li data-start="6485" data-end="6586"><strong data-start="6487" data-end="6500">Scenario:</strong> Replace rice in your stir-fries or casseroles with quinoa for an extra protein boost.</li><li data-start="6589" data-end="6664"><strong data-start="6591" data-end="6599">Tip:</strong> Use quinoa as a base and add lean proteins like chicken or tofu.</li></ul></li></ul><h3 data-start="6666" data-end="6701"><span class="ez-toc-section" id="Korralu_When_and_How_to_Use_It"></span>Korralu: When and How to Use It<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h3><ul data-start="6703" data-end="7364"><li data-start="6703" data-end="6930"><p data-start="6705" data-end="6730"><strong data-start="6705" data-end="6730">Traditional Porridge:</strong></p><ul data-start="6733" data-end="6930"><li data-start="6733" data-end="6848"><strong data-start="6735" data-end="6748">Scenario:</strong> If you enjoy traditional flavors, cook korralu as a thick porridge with a pinch of salt and spices.</li><li data-start="6851" data-end="6930"><strong data-start="6853" data-end="6861">Tip:</strong> Slow-cook in water or milk and add nuts or dried fruits for texture.</li></ul></li><li data-start="6932" data-end="7127"><p data-start="6934" data-end="6950"><strong data-start="6934" data-end="6950">Baked Goods:</strong></p><ul data-start="6953" data-end="7127"><li data-start="6953" data-end="7049"><strong data-start="6955" data-end="6968">Scenario:</strong> For home bakers, grind korralu into flour to make flatbreads or healthy muffins.</li><li data-start="7052" data-end="7127"><strong data-start="7054" data-end="7062">Tip:</strong> Mix with other whole-grain flours to balance texture and flavor.</li></ul></li><li data-start="7129" data-end="7364"><p data-start="7131" data-end="7151"><strong data-start="7131" data-end="7151">Cultural Dishes:</strong></p><ul data-start="7154" data-end="7364"><li data-start="7154" data-end="7283"><strong data-start="7156" data-end="7169">Scenario:</strong> If you’re exploring ethnic cuisines, incorporate korralu into Indian-style pilafs or Southeast Asian side dishes.</li><li data-start="7286" data-end="7364"><strong data-start="7288" data-end="7296">Tip:</strong> Pair with legumes and vegetables to create a balanced, low-GI meal.</li></ul></li></ul><hr data-start="7366" data-end="7369" /><h2 data-start="7371" data-end="7421"><span class="ez-toc-section" id="6_Health_Recommendations_Scientific_Evidence"></span>6. Health Recommendations &amp; Scientific Evidence<span class="ez-toc-section-end"></span></h2><p data-start="7423" data-end="7552">Incorporating both grains into your diet can offer a wide range of health benefits. Here are some science-backed recommendations:</p><ul data-start="7554" data-end="8790"><li data-start="7554" data-end="7874"><p data-start="7556" data-end="7580"><strong data-start="7556" data-end="7580">Blood Sugar Control:</strong></p><ul data-start="7583" data-end="7874"><li data-start="7583" data-end="7738"><strong data-start="7585" data-end="7598">Evidence:</strong> Studies published in the <em data-start="7624" data-end="7664">American Journal of Clinical Nutrition</em> indicate that low glycemic index foods help stabilize blood sugar levels.</li><li data-start="7741" data-end="7874"><strong data-start="7743" data-end="7754">Advice:</strong> If you’re diabetic or pre-diabetic, consider incorporating korralu, which is associated with a lower glycemic response.</li></ul></li><li data-start="7876" data-end="8185"><p data-start="7878" data-end="7895"><strong data-start="7878" data-end="7895">Heart Health:</strong></p><ul data-start="7898" data-end="8185"><li data-start="7898" data-end="8058"><strong data-start="7900" data-end="7913">Evidence:</strong> Research in journals like <em data-start="7940" data-end="7960">Nutrition Research</em> suggests that the unsaturated fats and antioxidants in quinoa can help reduce cholesterol levels.</li><li data-start="8061" data-end="8185"><strong data-start="8063" data-end="8074">Advice:</strong> Use quinoa in moderation if you’re watching your fat intake; pair it with vegetables for a heart-healthy meal.</li></ul></li><li data-start="8187" data-end="8473"><p data-start="8189" data-end="8210"><strong data-start="8189" data-end="8210">Digestive Health:</strong></p><ul data-start="8213" data-end="8473"><li data-start="8213" data-end="8372"><strong data-start="8215" data-end="8228">Evidence:</strong> High dietary fiber, as found in both quinoa and korralu, aids digestion and supports a healthy gut microbiome (source: <em data-start="8348" data-end="8370">Journal of Nutrition</em>).</li><li data-start="8375" data-end="8473"><strong data-start="8377" data-end="8388">Advice:</strong> Combine these grains with fermented foods like yogurt or kimchi to boost gut health.</li></ul></li><li data-start="8475" data-end="8790"><p data-start="8477" data-end="8505"><strong data-start="8477" data-end="8505">Overall Dietary Balance:</strong></p><ul data-start="8508" data-end="8790"><li data-start="8508" data-end="8642"><strong data-start="8510" data-end="8521">Advice:</strong> A varied diet that includes both quinoa and korralu can help you achieve a balance of macronutrients and micronutrients.</li><li data-start="8645" data-end="8790"><strong data-start="8647" data-end="8670">Serving Suggestion:</strong> Aim for 30–50 grams per serving of these grains as part of a meal, and mix with vegetables, legumes, and lean proteins.</li></ul></li></ul><hr /><p data-start="9513" data-end="9766">Remember, balancing ancient grains in your diet is supported by numerous studies (e.g., those published in the <em data-start="9624" data-end="9664">American Journal of Clinical Nutrition</em> and <em data-start="9669" data-end="9691">Journal of Nutrition</em>).</p>`} />
